All other components (frontend, consul, jaeger, memcached‑*, mongodb‑*, search) 
are Running and Ready with no error events.                                     

Root cause                                                                      

 • The six micro‑services above all attempt to open a MongoDB connection at     
   start‑up (Read database URL: mongodb‑<service>:27017).                       
 • Their logs show a panic: no reachable servers caused by the DB client failing
   to find a reachable MongoDB instance.                                        
 • The MongoDB pods for the corresponding databases were created after the      
   dependent services (see creation timestamps: e.g., mongodb‑geo 05:12:39 vs.  
   geo 05:11:10).                                                               
 • Because the services have no start‑up delay or readiness check for the DB,   
   they crash, trigger a BackOff restart loop, and only recover once the DB pod 
   becomes ready (as shown by the second successful start in each log).         

Fix recommendation                                                              

 1 Add a start‑up dependency check – use an init‑container or a startup probe   
   that waits for the corresponding MongoDB service to be reachable (e.g., nc -z
   mongodb‑geo 27017 with retries).                                             
 2 Expose MongoDB readiness – ensure each MongoDB pod has a proper readiness    
   probe so other pods can reliably wait for it.                                
 3 Adjust deployment ordering – if using Helm, add helm.sh/hook: pre-install    
   hooks or dependsOn annotations to start DB pods before the micro‑services.   
 4 Increase restart back‑off limits (optional) to avoid rapid crash loops while 
   the DB is still provisioning.                                                

Implementing any of the above will prevent the “no reachable servers” panic and 
eliminate the BackOff restarts observed.                                        

